  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Strong to severe thunderstorms this afternoon and evening\n   across north-central Idaho, tracking toward the Montana state\n   line. Hazards include gusts up to 60 mph and up to 1-inch \n   hail.\n\n-  Elevated fire weather (hot, dry, breezy) starting Friday and lasting\n   into the weekend region-wide.\n\n-  Worsening wildfire smoke degrading air quality and reducing \n   visibility.\n\nThe region remains on the western periphery of a large- scale \nupper-level ridge. Persistent synoptic descent and subsidence \naloft will maintain, hot, dry, and smoky conditions with no \nwidespread precipitation anticipated through the weekend.\n\nDespite broad subsidence, localized thermodynamic forcing will\nhelp to trigger strong to severe convection today (1:00 PM - 10:00\nPM). Effective shear (~40 kt), modest CAPE (~1,000 J/kg), and \nhigh DCAPE (~1,500 J/kg) will support organized discrete storms \ninitiating near the Snake River canyon in north-central Idaho \ntracking northeastward to the ID/MT border. The strongest storms \nin this environment will be capable of producing damaging winds up\nto 60 mph and hail up to 1-inch in diameter. Climatologically \nhigh moisture availability (PWATS > 1 inch) will yield brief \nheavy downpours, but rapid storm movement will prevent concerns \nfor flash flooding. There is a 10-20% chance that decaying storms \nreach the I-90 corridor late this evening which may bring a gusty\noutflow threat to the area.\n\nBreezy southwesterly winds Friday through Sunday will combine with\nlow humidity and hot temperatures to elevate fire weather\nconcerns. Red Flag Warnings are not currently being issued due to\nlive fuels and soil moisture remaining relatively high for this\ntime of year and wind speeds only on the edge of what would be an\nimmediate concern for fire weather products. Nonetheless,\nelevated fire weather conditions and increased active fire \nbehavior is to remain expected on any active burns or new \nignitions.\n\nDeep SW flow will continually transport Washington and Oregon\nwildfire smoke into ID/MT, maintaining poor air quality and \ndecreased visibilities across the region. No Heat Advisories are \nplanned as of yet due to overnight temperature recovery and \nshading from smoke limiting absolute maximum temperatures during \nthe peak of the afternoon hours.",
